ons place: 3 (first) + 4 (second) = 7. Write 7.\n\nTen-millions place: 5 (first) + 6 (second) = 11. Write 1, carry over 1.\n\nHundred-millions place: 5 (first) + 6 (second) + 1 (carry) = 12. Write 2 and carry over 1, but since there are no more digits, we need to add that 1 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together with the carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 7\n\nTens:8\n\nHundreds:1 (with carry 1)\n\nThousands:1 (with carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands:9\n\nHundred-thousands:3\n\nMillions:7\n\nTen-millions:1 (with carry 1)\n\nHundred-millions:2 (with carry 1 from the previous)\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 at the billions place.\n\nSo the total digits would be 1 followed by 2, then the rest. Let me reconstruct:\n\nThe carryover at the hundred-millions place addition (5+6+1=12) gives 2 in that place and a carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. Since both original numbers are 9-digit, the sum could be a 10-digit number. The final carryover makes it 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from right to left would be:\n\n7 (ones), 8 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 9 (ten-thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (hundred millions), and 1 (billions). \n\nPutting it all together from left to right: 1 2 1 7 3 9 1 1 8 7. Wait, that might be confusing. Let me write it step by step:\n\nStarting from the highest digit (billions):\n\nBillions place: 1\n\nHundred millions: 2\n\nTen millions: 1\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nWait, that seems off. Let me check again step by step.\n\nWait, let me do the addition step by step with each column:\n\nLet me write the numbers again aligned properly:\n\n          5 5 3 2 3 3 9 4 2\n        + 6 4 5 1 5 7 2 4 5\n        ---------------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1, ones):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 2 +5 =7. Write 7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4 +4=8. Write 8.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):9 +2=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):3 +7=10, plus carryover 1 makes 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3 +5=8, plus carryover 1 makes 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):2 +1=3. Write 3.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):3 +4=7. Write7.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):5 +6=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5 +6=11, plus carryover1 makes 12. So write 2 and carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place), so we add a 1 at the beginning.\n\nPutting all together from left to right (billions, hundred millions, ..., ones):\n\n1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,217,391,187?\n\nWait, let me verify with actual addition:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, let's compute 553,233,942 + 600,000,000 = 1,153,233,942\n\nThen add the remaining 45,157,245:\n\n1,153,233,942 + 45,157,245.\n\nAdding 45,000,000 gives 1,198,233,942\n\nThen adding 157,245:\n\n1,198,233,942 + 157,245 = 1,198,391,187.\n\nWait, but that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, perhaps my digit-by-digit approach had an error. Let me check again.\n\nWait, in the digit positions:\n\nWhen adding the hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 5, second is 6. So 5+6=11, plus the carryover from the ten millions place?\n\nWait, let's go through each digit again carefully.\n\nLet me write down each digit's place step by step:\n\nStarting from the right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\n2 +5 =7. No carryover. Result digit 7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n4 +4=8. Result 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n9 +2=11. Result 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n3 (from first number) +7 (second) =10 + carryover 1 =11. Result 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n3 +5 =8 + carryover1=9. Result9, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n2 +1=3. Result3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n3 +4=7. Result7.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n5 +6=11. Result1, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n5 +6=11 + carryover1=12. Result2, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from position9 must be added as the next digit (billions place), so the total is 1 followed by the previous digits.\n\nSo the total is:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:7\n\nSo combining these digits from left to right: 1 2 1 7 3 9 1 1 8 7 \u2192 1,217,391,187.\n\nBut when I did the other method, I got 1,198,391,187. Wait, there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's do the addition numerically:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nFirst number: 553,233,942\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nLet me write them out:\n\n553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\nStart adding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n2 +5 =7\n\n4 +4=8\n\n9+2=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\n3+7 +1=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\n3+5 +1=9 \u2192 9, no carryover\n\n2+1=3\n\n3+4=7\n\n5+6=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\n5+6 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nThe carryover1 becomes the next digit, so total is 1,217,391,187.\n\nWait, so that's correct. But when I did the other way:\n\n553,233,942 + 645,157,245.\n\nLet me add 553,233,942 + 645,157,245:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n553,233,942 + 600,000,000 = 1,153,233,942\n\nThen add 45,157,245 to that:\n\n1,153,233,942 + 45,000,000 = 1,198,233,942\n\nThen add 157,245:\n\n1,198,233,942 + 157,245 = ?\n\n1,198,233,942 + 100,000 = 1,298,233,942? Wait, no, that can't be right. Wait, no:\n\nWait, 1,153,233,942 + 645,157,245. Let me try another method.\n\nAlternatively, compute 553,233,942 + 645,157,245:\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n553,233,942 is 553 million plus 233,942\n\n645,157,245 is 645 million plus 157,245\n\nAdding the millions: 553 + 645 = 1,198 million\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 233,942 + 157,245 = ?\n\n233,942 + 157,245:\n\n200,000 + 100,000 = 300,000\n\n33,942 +57,245 = 91,187\n\nSo total is 300,000 +91,187 = 391,187.\n\nThus total sum is 1,198,000,000 + 391,187 = 1,198,391,187.\n\nWait a minute, this contradicts the previous digit-wise result of 1,217,391,187. So which is correct?\n\nHmm, I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nLet me do the addition more carefully:\n\nFirst number: 553,233,942\n\nSecond number:645,157,245\n\nAdding:\n\nLet me write them as:\n\n  553,233,942\n\n+645,157,245\n\n----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 2 +5 =7\n\nTens:4 +4=8\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1\n\nThousands:3+7=10 +1=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929\n\nHundred thousands:2+1=3\n\nMillions:3+4=7\n\nTen millions:5+6=11 \u2192 write1, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:5+6=11 +1=12 \u2192 write2, carryover1\n\nFinally, the carryover1 \u2192 so the digits are 1 2 1 7 3 9 1 1 8 7 \u2192 1,217,391,187.\n\nWait but the other method gave 1,198,391,187. Where's the error?\n\nWait, in the second method:\n\n553,233,942 +645,157,245.\n\nLet me compute 553,233,942 + 645,157,245:\n\nFirst, 500,000,000 +600,000,000 =1,100,000,000\n\n53,233,942 +45,157,245 = ?\n\n53,233,942 +40,000,000 =93,233,942\n\nAdd 5,157,245: 93,233,942 +5,000,000 =98,233,942\n\n+157,245 \u2192 98,233,942 +157,245 = 98,391,187\n\nSo total is 1,100,000,000 +98,391,187 =1,198,391,187.\n\nWait, so now there's a conflict between the two methods. Which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in digit positions in the first method.\n\nWait, let's check the hundred millions place again.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit: the first digit is 5 (since it's 553 million... so 5 is hundred millions, 5 is ten millions, etc. Let's confirm the digit positions.\n\nWait, the first number is 553,233,942:\n\nBreaking