27, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (tens):4+4=8 \u21928, no carryover.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+2=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3+7=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+5=8 +1=9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2+1=3 \u21923.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3+4=7 \u21927.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5+6=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+6=11 +1 (carryover) =12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nNow, the final carryover1 is added as a new digit (billions place).\n\nThus the digits from left (billions) to right (ones) are:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n1 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n7 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,217,391,187.\n\nBut in the other method, I got 1,198,391,187. There's a discrepancy in the millions and ten millions places.\n\nWait, let me check the millions place again.\n\nPosition7 (millions) in first number is 3 (since 553,233,942: the millions digit is the 3 in 553 million).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 5 (from 645,157,245: the 5 in 645 million).\n\nWait, wait a second! Here's the mistake!\n\nWait, in the second number: 645,157,245 is broken down as 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 5 (millions), then 1 (hundred thousands), etc. So the millions digit is 5. \n\nTherefore, in position7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number: 3\n\nSecond number:5\n\nSo 3+5=8, not 3+4. Wait, where did the 4 come from?\n\nWait, in my initial digit breakdown for the second number, I might have made an error.\n\nWait, let me re-express the second number:\n\n645,157,245.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 6 hundred millions\n\n- 4 ten millions\n\n- 5 millions\n\n- 1 hundred thousands\n\n- 5 ten thousands\n\n- 7 thousands\n\n- 2 hundreds\n\n- 4 tens\n\n-5 ones.\n\nSo the millions digit is indeed 5. Therefore, position7 (millions) in the second number is 5, not 4. That was my mistake earlier!\n\nSo