en millions)  \n- 8 (millions)  \n- 9 (hundred thousands)  \n- 6 (ten thousands)  \n- 2 (thousands)  \n- 3 (hundreds)  \n- 1 (tens)  \n- 7 (ones)\n\nWait, actually, the standard breakdown for 878,962,317:\n\nDigits from right:  \n7 (ones), 1 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 2 (thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 8 (millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions). \n\nSecond number: 143,379,661  \nDigits from right:  \n1 (ones), 6 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, actually, the second number is 143,379,661:\n\nBreaking down:  \n1 (ones), 6 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions). \n\nSo when adding:\n\nLet me align them properly:\n\n```\n          HUNDRED MILLIONS TEN MILLIONS MILLIONS HUNDRED THOUSANDS TEN THOUSANDS THOUSANDS HUNDREDS TENS ONES\nFirst number:           8           7         8            9           6         2      3     1   7\nSecond number:          1           3         4            3           7         9      6     6   1\n```\n\nWait, actually, perhaps writing them vertically:\n\nLet me write the numbers digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition 1 (Ones):  \n7 (from first) + 1 (from second) = 8\n\nPosition 2 (Tens):  \n1 + 6 =7\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):  \n3 +6 =9\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):  \n2 +9 =11 \u2192 Write 1, carryover 1\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):  \n6 +7 =13, plus carryover 1 \u2192 14 \u2192 Write 4, carryover 1\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):  \n9 +3 =12, plus 1 \u219213 \u2192 Write 3, carryover 1\n\nPosition7 (Millions):  \n8 +4 =12, plus 1 \u219213 \u2192 Write 3, carryover 1? Wait, hold on, wait, the first number's millions digit is 8, the second number's millions digit is 4? Let me check again.\n\nWait the first number is 878,962,317:  \nBreaking down each digit from right:\n\nPosition 1 (Ones): 7  \nPosition2 (Tens):1  \nPosition3 (Hundreds):3  \nPosition4 (Thousands):2  \nPosition5 (Ten thousands):6  \nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):9  \nPosition7 (Millions):8  \nPosition8 (Ten millions):7  \nPosition9 (Hundred millions):8  \n\nSecond number:143,379,661:  \nPosition1 (Ones):1  \nPosition2 (Tens):6  \nPosition3 (Hundreds):6  \nPosition4 (Thousands):9  \nPosition5 (Ten thousands):7  \nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):3  \nPosition7 (Millions):4  \nPosition8 (Ten millions):3  \nPosition9 (Hundred millions):1  \n\nSo the millions place (position7): first number 8, second number 4. So 8+4 =12 plus any carryover?\n\nWait let's redo step by step with correct positions:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones (position1):  \n7 +1 =8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens (position2):  \n1 +6 =7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds (position3):  \n3 +6 =9. Write 9. No carryover.\n\n4. Thousands (position4):  \n2 +9 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands (position5):  \n6 +7 =13 +1 (carryover) =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands (position6):  \n9 +3 =12 +1 (carryover) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions (position7):  \n8 +4 =12 +1 (carryover) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions (position8):  \n7 (from first number) +3 (from second) =10 +1 (carryover) =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n9. Hundred millions (position9):  \n8 +1 =9 +1 (carryover) =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, we have to add that final carryover of 1 as a new digit to the left, making it a 1 followed by the rest.\n\nPutting all digits together from highest to lowest:\n\nStarting with the carryover 1, then the hundred millions place (0), ten millions (1), millions (3), hundred thousands (3), ten thousands (4), thousands (1), hundreds (9), tens (7), ones (8).\n\nWait, let's list each position:\n\nAfter all steps:\n\n- The carryover after hundred millions place is 1, so that becomes the next digit (billions place). So the digits are:\n\nBillions:1  \nHundred millions:0  \nTen millions:1  \nMillions:3  \nHundred thousands:3  \nTen thousands:4  \nThousands:1  \nHundreds:9  \nTens:7  \nOnes:8\n\nSo combining all these digits from left to right (billions to ones):\n\n1 0 1 3 3 4 1 9 7 8.\n\nWait that would be 1,013,341,978? Let me check:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nBillions:1  \nHundred millions:0  \nTen millions:1 \u2192 so 100,000,000 + 10,000,000 = 110,000,000  \nMillions:3 \u2192 3,000,000 \u2192 total so far 113,000,000  \nHundred thousands:3 \u2192 300,000 \u2192 113,300,000  \nTen thousands:4 \u2192 40,000 \u2192 113,340,000  \nThousands:1 \u2192 1,000 \u2192 113,341,000  \nHundreds:9 \u2192 900 \u2192 113,341,900  \nTens:7 \u219270 \u2192113,341,970  \nOnes:8 \u2192113,341,978.\n\nWait but the total sum should be 878,962,317 +143,379,661. Let me compute that using another method to verify.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\nFirst number: 878,962,317  \nSecond number:143,379,661  \nAdding them:\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n878,962,317  \n+143,379,661  \n= ?\n\nAdding step by step:\n\nStart with 800,000,000 + 100,000,000 = 900,000,000  \nThen 78,962,317 +43,379,661.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking into parts:\n\n878,962,317  \n+143,379,661  \n= (800,000,000 + 70,000,000 + 8,000,000 + 900,000 + 60,000 + 2,000 + 300 + 10 +7)  \n+ (100,000,000 + 40,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 300,000 +70,000 +9,000 +600 +60 +1)\n\nAdding term by term:\n\nBillions: 800M + 100M = 900M  \nHundred millions: 70M +40M =110M  \nTen millions:8M +3M=11M  \nMillions: (already included in above?) Wait, maybe better to just add normally.\n\nAlternatively, let me use calculator-like addition:\n\n878,962,317  \n+143,379,661  \nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:7+1=8  \nTens:1+6=7  \nHundreds:3+6=9  \nThousands:2+9=11 (carryover 1)  \nTen thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 (carryover 1)  \nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13 (carryover1)  \nMillions:8+4=12 +1=13 (carryover1)  \nTen millions:7+3=10 +1=11 (carryover1)  \nHundred millions:8+1=9 +1=10 (carryover1)  \nNow, the final carryover1 makes it a new digit.\n\nSo the digits as per previous calculation: 1,0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),9 (hundreds),7 (tens),8 (ones). So 1,013,341,978?\n\nWait but when I do 878,962,317 +143,379,661:\n\nLet me compute:\n\n878,962,317  \n+143,379,661  \n= ?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with 878,962,317 + 100,000,000 = 978,962,317  \nThen add 43,379,661:  \n978,962,317 +40,000,000 =1,018,962,317  \n+3,379,661 = ?\n\n1,018,962,317 +3,000,000 =1,021,962,317  \n+379,661 =1,021,962,317 + 300,000 =1,022,262,317  \n+79,661 =1,022,262,317 +70,000 =1,022,332,317  \n+9,661 =1,022,341,978.\n\nAh! So the correct total is 1,022,341,978. Wait, but according to my previous digit-by-digit addition I had 1,013,341,978, which is different. There's a discrepancy here. I must have made a mistake in the digit-by-digit steps.\n\nLet me retrace the steps again carefully.\n\nLet me write the numbers aligned properly:\n\nFirst number: 878,962,317  \nSecond number:143,379,661  \nAdding:\n\nLet me write them without commas for clarity:\n\nFirst: 8 7 8 9 6 2 3 1 7  \nSecond:1 4 3 3 7 9 6 6 1  \nWait, actually, no. Wait the numbers are 878,962,317 and 143,379,661. Let's write each digit in order from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number (878,962,317):\n\nPosition 1 (Ones):7  \nPosition2 (Tens):1  \nPosition3 (Hundreds):3  \nPosition4 (Thousands):2  \nPosition5 (Ten thousands):6  \nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):9  \nPosition7 (Millions):8  \nPosition8 (Ten millions):7  \nPosition9 (Hundred millions):8  \n\nSecond number (143,379,661):\n\nPosition1 (Ones):1  \nPosition2 (Tens):6  \nPosition3 (Hundreds):6  \nPosition4 (Thousands):9  \nPosition5 (Ten thousands):7  \nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):3  \nPosition7 (Millions):4  \nPosition8 (Ten millions):3  \nPosition9 (Hundred millions):1  \n\nNow, adding each position step by step with carries:\n\nStarting from Position1 (Ones):\n\n1. **Ones (Position1):**  \n7 +1 =8 \u2192 Write 8, carryover 0.\n\n2. **Tens (Position2):**  \n1 +6 =7 \u2192 Write 7, carryover 0.\n\n3. **Hundreds (Position3):**  \n3 +6 =9 \u2192 Write 9, carryover 0.\n\n4. **Thousands (Position4):**  \n2 +9 =11 \u2192 Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n5. **Ten thousands (Position5):**  \n6 +7 =13 +1 (carryover) =14 \u2192 Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n6. **Hundred thousands (Position6):**  \n9 +3 =12 +1 (carryover) =13 \u2192 Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n7. **Millions (Position7):**  \n8 +4 =12 +1 (carryover) =13 \u2192 Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n8. **Ten millions (Position8):**  \n7 +3 =10 +1 (carryover) =11 \u2192 Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n9. **Hundred millions (Position9):**  \n8 +1 =9 +1 (carryover) =10 \u2192 Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions place, we add a new digit to the left (billions place):\n\n10. **Billions place (new):**  \nCarryover 1 \u2192 Write 1.\n\nNow, compiling all the digits from highest to lowest (billions down to ones):\n\nBillions:1  \nHundred millions:0  \nTen millions:1  \nMillions:3  \nHundred thousands:3  \nTen thousands:4  \nThousands:1  \nHundreds:9  \nTens:7  \nOnes:8  \n\nSo the number is 1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones). \n\nPutting that together: 1,013,341,978. But earlier when I did the breakdown step-by-step addition, I got 1,022,341,978. Wait, that's conflicting. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me compute using another approach:\n\nFirst number: 878,962,317  \nSecond number:143,379,661  \nAdding them:\n\nLet me add 878,962,317 + 143,379,661.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n878,962,317  \n+143,379,661  \n= ?\n\nLet me add 800,000,000 + 100,000,000 = 900,000,000  \nThen 78,962,317 +43,379,661:\n\n78,962,317 +40,000,000 =118,962,317  \n+3,379,661 = 118,962,317 +3,000,000 =121,962,317  \n+379,661 =121,962,317 +300,000 =122,262,317  \n+79,661 =122,262,317 +70,000 =122,332,317  \n+9,661 =122,341,978.\n\nAdding to the 900,000,000 gives 900,000,000 +122,341,978 =1,022,341,978.\n\nAh! So the correct total is 1,022,341,978. But according to the digit-by-digit method earlier, I got 1,013,341,978. There's a mistake here. Let me check where I went wrong in the digit-by-digit steps.\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nAt the Ten millions place (Position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 7, second number's is 3. So 7+3=10, plus carryover from the millions place (which was 1). So 10+1=11. Therefore, write down 1 and carryover 1. That's correct.\n\nThen, the Hundred millions place (Position9): first number is 8, second is 1. 8+1=9 plus carryover 1 =10. So write 0 and carryover 1. Correct.\n\nThen the billions place is 1. So total is 1 followed by 0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), etc. But that would make the number 1,013,341,978. Wait, but according to the other method it's 1,022,341,978. There's a discrepancy in the ten millions and millions digits.\n\nWait, let me check the millions place again.\n\nPosition7 (Millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 8, second is 4. 8+4=12 plus carryover 1 (from hundred thousands place which was 1) \u219213. So write 3, carryover 1. That's correct.\n\nSo the millions digit is 3. Then ten millions place:\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):\n\n7 +3 =10 plus carryover 1 \u219211. So ten millions digit is 1, carryover 1.\n\nSo the ten millions place is 1, but in the total sum from the other method, it should be 2 in the ten millions place?\n\nWait let me look at the total number from the correct calculation:1,022,341,978.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,022,341,978:\n\n- Billions:1  \n- Hundred millions:0  \n- Ten millions:2  \n- Millions:2  \n- Hundred thousands:3  \n- Ten thousands:4  \n- Thousands:1  \n- Hundreds:9  \n- Tens:7  \n- Ones:8  \n\nWait a second! The ten millions digit here is 2, but in the digit-by-digit method, I had 1. That